("The `Child` element is a new object with a new `props` object (`{ title: \"Static\" }`), so the cheap identity check (\"same props object and no pending work?\") fails, and — `Child` not being memoized — React calls `Child`. `Child` returns another new element tree, which reconciles against its existing fibers and discovers *no differences*: no flags are set. In the commit phase, React visits only the fibers that have flags: the button's text node (and possibly the button fiber for its changed handler, which touches no DOM). `Child`'s DOM nodes are never visited.",
 "The `Child` element is a new object with a new `props` object (`{ title: \"Static\" }`), so the cheap identity check (\"same props object and no pending work?\") fails, and — `Child` not being memoized — React calls `Child`. `Child` returns another new element tree, which reconciles against its existing fibers with new props objects. In current React the host fibers (`article`, `h3`, `p`) are flagged for update merely because their props *objects* changed; the commit then calls `commitUpdate`, which diffs old vs new props, finds nothing different, and writes nothing to the DOM (executed: their stored-props records were replaced, no mutation record appeared). In the commit phase the visible DOM work is the button's text node (the button fiber is also flagged for its changed handler, which only refreshes stored props). `Child`'s DOM nodes are *visited cheaply but never written*."),
